Z||000000||ka4nkf||11-17-2001||09:45 PM||||Does anyone know why they do not put a drain plug in the gas tank, also the auto tranny?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Jake_2m4||11-17-2001||10:06 PM||||Because it would be easier to steal your gas.<P>------------------<BR>'85 Fiero SC<BR>'95 Grand Am SE||||reg||6
Z||000002||Bazooka||11-17-2001||10:29 PM||||The gastank is cleaned and filtered by the intake sock (screen) and the inside of the tank remains fairly spotless in normal use. A plug on the tank would create potential fire hazards since the vapors in an empty tank are very flammable. The water that condenses in it can be pumped out thru the injectors with no change in operation, in contrast to a diesel engine. Any repairs or maintenance should be done by tank removal. <P>     On the tranny pan drain plug, I think it goes back to manufacturing costs. Trans maintenance usually means changing the filter and the fluid and cleaning at the same time and so the entire pan should be removed. If not, the maintenance would be useless. It would be a neater job to drain the tranny thru a plug, but not so economical. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Bazooka (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ka4nkf||11-17-2001||11:47 PM||||Bazooka<BR>I agree with you on the gas tank but it would be a lot easier to take the pan off of the tranny if you could drain it first. the way it is now you just can't get it off without making a mess.||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||11-18-2001||01:20 PM||||Question #1: Because it's cheaper to not have a drain plug.  How many times does the average car owner drain his fuel tank (other than by running out of gas)?  I imagine the answer is: Approximately zero.<P>Question #2:  Because it's cheaper to not have a drain plug.  How many times is the average car owner supposed to change his tranny fluid?  Approximately 2 times per vehicle lifetime. Yes, it can be a mess - but it's something GM probably judged that most people are willing to put up with, every 50,000 miles or more.   How many times does the average car owner actually do it? Approximately zero.<P>Strictly opinions, mind you, based upon very little data  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">   You asked... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||ka4nkf||11-20-2001||03:32 AM||||StuGood<BR>I agree with you but haow many times does anyone change the standard tranny fluid or the rear end fluid? Both have drain plugs.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Formula88||11-20-2001||12:55 PM||||Well, manual trannys don't have an oil pan, so the only way to drain them is with a drain plug.  No filter to change, either, so no pan is necessary. <P>As for rear ends?  Well, I guess with the verticle pan gasket, they figured the mess would warrant a drain plug?  Not really sure.||||reg||1
